CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ specialization: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ __is_same: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ u8 string literal: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ u8 character literal: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.5\] line \d+ underlying: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.6\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [basic.fundamental]/9: char8_t is a distinct type with underlying
type unsigned char; [lex.ccon]/3, [lex.string]/6: u8'a' has type char8_t
and u8"a" type const char8_t[2].  The parser turned char8_t into
unsigned char, and c_preprocess.cpp undefined __cpp_char8_t so that the
library would not declare duplicate specializations; kind<unsigned char>
selected kind<char8_t> (bin53).  char8_t is now its own bit-vector type
(#c_type char8_t, cpp11_specialization_distinguishes_fundamental_types),
u8 literals have it in C++20, and __cpp_char8_t is defined as the
compiler defines it.  g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept this program, and it
fails only its WRONG assertion.
